  "notes": "West leads the ♠K against 4♥. West cashes 2 top spades and continues a third round, East ruffing. East returns a diamond, South should draw the remaining trumps and concentrate on coping with his club loser.\n\nIf diamonds are 3-3, there will be no problem as North's long diamond will take care of South's club loser. However, it is likely that East has a diamond guard. In this case, a squeeze will be South's only chance.\n\nWest's overcall marks him with the ♣K, leaving the minor suit guards divided. However, if East has the ♣J, South can succeed. The ♣Q is played, covered by the ♣K, and won with the ♣A. This play in clubs is known as transferring the threat. By forcing West to cover the ♣Q, the sole guard of the club suit has been transferred to East's ♣J.\n\nOn the play of the last heart, the ♣2 is discarded from dummy and East is squeezed. Discarding the ♣J will establish South's ♣10 and discarding a diamond will set up a long diamond trick in dummy.",
